<!--
    GH-881 part B / GH-866: a call argument the caller also tests is dropped.

        int t9(int *p) { int x = *p + 2; if (x) return ext(x) + 1; return 5; }

    gcc -O2 computes x straight into the argument register and tests it there
    (lea 0x2(%rax),%edi; test %edi,%edi; je; call ext), and kuna printed
    `return ext() + 1;`.  gcc's `int fwd(int *p) { int x = *p; if (x > 5)
    return x; return ext(x); }` loads x into edi, compares it, returns it on
    one path and tail-calls ext on the other, and printed `ext()` the same way.
    Funcdata::onlyOpUse (funcdata_varnode.cc:1851) refuses a value that also
    feeds a CBRANCH or the other path's RETURN, so checkInputTrialUse leaves the
    edi trial inactive and nothing active keeps it in the list.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 active input-trial scoring.  The P0 assertion
    `option calleereadarg on|off` decides whether a call's settled argument
    list gains a refused register the caller wrote because the callee's own
    body (the bounded entry decode calleedeadarg takes, cached per callee)
    reads that register on entry.

    The fixture is hand-assembled SysV x86-64 (the gcc -O2 code for t9 and fwd
    with ext defined in the same image), plus two controls that keep the same
    caller shape:
      neg   leaves its tested x in edi and calls g, which reads no register:
            g() in both passes.
      negv  leaves its tested x in esi and tail-calls vlog, whose variadic
            register-save prologue reads every argument register through r9:
            vlog(msg) in both passes.  Reading the last argument register is
            no evidence of an argument, and the caller's xor %eax,%eax sets
            the call up as variadic; either declines it.
      sbbpick  leaves a tested sum in ecx and tail-calls cmpf, whose
            sbb %ecx,%ecx computes its result and flags from ecx compared
            with itself, which reads no value: cmpf(p[0],p[1]) in both passes.
      k1d   is clang -O2 g3(1, 0, x): clang leaves esi alone because g3 never
            reads it, so esi still holds the caller's own q.  g3 reads edx,
            but the gap in front of it is the caller's incoming register,
            which the callee does not read either, so nothing is added:
            g3(1) in both passes.

    Pass 1 (`option calleereadarg off`) reproduces the BUG: ext() at both t9
    and fwd.  Pass 2 (`option calleereadarg on`, the default) is the FIX:
    ext(x) at both, and the two controls are unchanged.
-->
